From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nfqueue: activate a batch's direct flows together, and arm
 only in time

The commit pump arms a batch of direct flows under one backend
acquisition, then activated them one at a time: each flow accepted its
held originals, took the backend write lock again and updated its
conn state in a blocking section of its own. A batch of N direct flows
thus waited N times behind whatever held the backend meanwhile (the
janitor's scans hold it for reading), and the pump took no new commits
until the last activation. Under a sustained arrival of new direct UDP
flows, requests aged in the queue, the next batch was armed close to
its three-second hold, and an armed flow that missed it ended the
process ("DirectArmed flow lost its initializer before activation").
On an emulated NanoPi R4S at 200 new flows a second, a batch of 256
flows took 2.6 s to activate one by one, its oldest request already
3 s old; honk exited within minutes.

Activate the batch's armed flows together: accept each flow's held
originals off the backend lock, oldest first, then activate every flow
whose originals are out under one backend acquisition and one blocking
section; a flow whose originals arrived meanwhile accepts them and goes
round again, as before. Arm -> originals -> Activate stays in FIFO
order, and a failure after arming stays fatal.

An armed flow that misses its hold is fatal by design, so arm a flow
only with the time left for its activation, which comes after the rest
of the batch's commit and the release of the originals of every flow
the batch arms: its deadline less a 300 ms margin, the commit work of
the batch's requests still to visit, itself included, and the
activation work of the flows armed before it and of those still to
visit. The commit work goes at the slower of a moving average per
request, from the batch's first visit until its armed flows can be
activated, and the batch's own rate so far; the activation work at a
moving average per flow without the waits for the backend (a stall
must not inflate it). The reserve is at least half the hold: under load
the work and waits between arming and activation outgrow what the
measured work foretells, reaching a second on the emulator, where every
flow that missed its hold had been armed with less than that left. And
it is at most all of the hold but the margin, so that a flow fresh from
the queue is always armed and the averages stay current rather than
locking every direct flow out. A later flow expires
unarmed, without conn mutation, as a late request already does; its
originals are dropped and its client retries. Overload sheds new flows
instead of ending the process. Reserving the activation work of the
flows armed before it alone was not enough: under host load, a flow
visited early in a batch of 44 was armed with 365 ms left and missed
its hold while the 43 requests after it committed, 8 ms each, and
during a firewall reload one of a batch of 81, armed by the recorded
2.6 ms a request, missed it while the rest committed at 6.4 ms each and
the batch's 81 flows had their originals released.

Tests: direct flows decided together, while a scanner holds the backend
for reading 150 ms at a time, are all activated (unpatched: the later
ones fail with ArmedInProgress); a flow without time for its activation
expires unarmed, nothing armed and nothing fatal (unpatched: armed);
a flow fresh from the queue is armed however long commits and
activations took; a flow past half its hold expires unarmed however
little its batch's work (without the floor: armed); a flow visited
early in a batch reserves the commit
work of the requests after it (without that: armed); a batch slowed
down reserves at its own rate (by the recorded one: armed); a flow
reserves the activation work of the flows its batch may still arm (of
those before it alone: armed); and the reserve follows the recorded
work, up to its bound. The design document describes both.

Signed-off-by: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
